		content.push(localize('workbench.action.chat.focusInput', 'To focus the input box for chat requests, invoke the Focus Chat Input command{0}.', getChatFocusKeybindingLabel(keybindingService, type, 'input')));
		content.push(localize('chat.progressVerbosity', 'As the chat request is being processed, you will hear verbose progress updates if the request takes more than 4 seconds. This includes information like searched text for <search term> with X results, created file <file_name>, or read file <file path>. This can be disabled with accessibility.verboseChatProgressUpdates.'));
		content.push(localize('chat.persistentProgress', "The chat.experimental.persistentProgress setting defaults to Draw in all versions of VS Code. Experiments can override the default; an explicit setting takes precedence. Choose Draw, Draw (Monochrome), or Draw (Monochrome, No Icon) to keep a working progress indicator at the bottom until the response finishes. When the response finishes, its toolbar takes the indicator's place. Draw ties and unravels the VS Code mark with fast parabolic ribbon motion and a brief rest between loops. The No Icon variant hides only the decorative VS Code icon; progress text and spoken updates are unchanged. The chat.experimental.persistentProgressVerbosity setting defaults to Compact: tool calls preview while running, then collapse in place to a single summary row when thinking or response text resumes, or the response finishes. Choose Verbose to keep tool chains expanded without a group header or an internal scrolling limit. Choose Conversation to collapse reasoning and tool calls together into one section between response text; the section starts collapsed, its header names the current tool call, or the heading of the current reasoning summary (Thinking when the reasoning has no heading), while it runs, and reports how long it took once it finishes, such as Finished thinking after 5s, or Finished thinking when the time is unknown. Use Tab to focus a summary and press Enter or Space to expand or collapse its tool calls. Automatic collapse waits until keyboard focus leaves the group. Completed responses still follow chat.agent.collapseCompletedResponses. Except in Conversation, reasoning appears separately as collapsible previews; focus a reasoning header and press Enter or Space to expand or collapse it. Standalone tools keep their decorative icons. Terminal activity animations and subagent pills are unchanged. The indicator reports counts of running subagents and background commands, including work started in earlier requests, whether the main response is progressing or idle. Requests for your input, approval, plan review, or authentication take priority over these counts. After 90 seconds without response activity and with no active tools, progress tasks, or known background work, it reports that the response is taking longer than usual. Long-running foreground terminal commands do not trigger this message. Reduced motion disables the collapse animation and the Conversation header's transitions between steps, and keeps the indicator visible without animation. Spoken progress updates are controlled by accessibility.verboseChatProgressUpdates."));
		content.push(localize('chat.persistentReasoningTitles', "With persistent progress, reasoning headers say Thinking while content arrives. When reasoning finishes, a single heading becomes the header and is removed from the details. Multiple headings remain in the details, with a summary in the header when chat.agent.thinking.generateTitles is enabled and title generation is available. Otherwise, the header says Finished thinking, followed by how long it took when that is known."));
		content.push(localize('chat.persistentProgressHandoff', "While a response is running, automatically collapsing a reasoning or tool preview keeps a visible header in place. If the header is above the visible content but its details are visible, it moves below the pinned prompt, or to the top of the viewport when there is no pinned prompt. Only the space needed for the visible viewport is reserved. That space is removed when the response finishes or is stopped, after any pending transition and content rendering finish. Expanding a preview consumes reserved space as its content grows. Scrolling to the bottom removes the reserved space. Manual collapses do not add padding. The following response content appears after an automatic collapse finishes. Reduced motion skips this delay. If keyboard focus keeps a preview open, new content continues to appear without waiting for focus to move."));
		content.push(localize('chat.persistentProgressSimplifiedTools', "With Compact persistent progress, a single tool uses its normal rendering, icon, and details without a group summary or automatic group collapse. Group headers summarize up to three activity groups from Agent Host data, prioritizing edits and commands, without an additional model request. Remaining activity is counted as other steps; this counts tool calls and visible steps rather than unique files or search phrases. Expand the summary to inspect all calls. Repeated files and search phrases are counted once in named activity groups. Read summaries count files rather than requested lines. Edit summaries show filenames or file counts, with available additions and deletions in the existing diff badge. Failed, skipped, denied, and unfinished tool invocations remain explicit, including when folded into other steps. A completed shell invocation counts as a command even if its process continues in the background; group headers do not track later process status. Groups with no recognized activity or outcome retain a neutral step-count summary. MCP tools use the MCP icon. The working indicator reports when chat is waiting for your answers instead of showing a separate question-tool progress row. Question controls remain available, answered questions remain in the response, and question-tool failures remain visible."));
